docs/design/yang.typescript.md-99-### 2.2 Shared rulings
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-100-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-101-- **No new AST tag, no engine change, no codec change** for either language.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-102-  Everything is lowering arms, prelude code over `cell`, `data`, `integer` and
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-103-  `stream/poll!`, and composition.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-104-- **Each language's null is its own tagged sentinel** (`yang.antlr.md` 8.11).
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-105-  JavaScript has two, PHP has one. None is host `nil`; none is `:py/None`.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-106-- **Numbers**: Jing's existing kind distinction (exact integer versus float64
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-107-  content) is reused as the guest's numeric kind. The float64 carrier is
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-108-  mandatory on the JavaScript host for every float-kind value (`yang.antlr.md`
--
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-571-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-572-The name supply is `%<prefix><cst-id>` (`%` cannot begin a JS identifier).
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-573-Those names are canonical row slots (`:lambda [:params :syms]`), so inserting
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-574-an erased annotation would shift preorder CST ids and move rows. The ruling is
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-575-**canonical renaming before projection**, as a shared pass:
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-576-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-577-- `yang.core.names/canonicalize` walks the finished UAST in a deterministic
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-578-  preorder over the row grammar's slots and renames every symbol whose name
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-579-  begins with `%` to `%<n>` by first-occurrence order. It is capture-safe
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-580-  because `%` names are never guest names and are already unique per lowering.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-581-  `%_` (discard) and the reserved `yang.safepoint/_` are excluded.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-582-- The pass is a pure function of the lowered tree, and the tree is a function
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-583-  of the runtime constructs alone once type-only subtrees are consumed; hence
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-584-  rows are a function of the runtime tree.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-585-- The TS lowering keeps CST ids internally and applies the pass in
docs/design/yang.typescript.md:586:  `lower-packet`, `module-packet` and `module-spec`, before `mark-tails` and
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-587-  projection. Site marks (metadata) survive the rename, and the safepoint
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-588-  stage reconstructs from rows that already carry canonical names.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-589-- Python is not changed: the pass is not applied to Python in L2. Adopting it
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-590-  there is an owner-scheduled golden-moving slice with no correctness need
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-591-  (owner decision A12.8).
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-592-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-593-## 7. Scope analysis
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-594-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-595-`yang.typescript.antlr.scope`. Hoisting is the whole job:
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-596-
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-597-- Per function scope: `var` and function declarations are hoisted to the
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-598-  function top; their cells are allocated with `:js/undefined`, and functions
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-599-  are initialized first.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-600-- Per block: `let`, `const` and `class` cells are allocated with `:js/tdz`.
docs/design/yang.typescript.md-601-- `for (let ...)` gets a fresh cell per iteration, closed over correctly.
